 "title": "Detect Squares",
 "slug": "detect-squares",
 "category": "math-geometry",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/detect-squares/",
 "pattern": "Hash map of points; fix one side, look up the other two corners",
 "statement": "Design a structure that receives points on the plane. `add(point)` stores a point (duplicates are allowed and count separately). `count(point)` returns how many **axis-aligned squares** with positive area can be formed using the query point and three stored points.",
 "example": {
  "input": "DetectSquares()\nadd([3, 10])\nadd([11, 2])\nadd([3, 2])\ncount([11, 10])  →  1\ncount([14, 8])  →  0\nadd([11, 2])\ncount([11, 10])  →  2",
  "output": "",
  "why": "(11, 10) completes a square with the three stored points. Adding (11, 2) a second time makes two such squares."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "DetectSquares()\nadd([0, 0])\nadd([0, 2])\nadd([2, 0])\ncount([2, 2])  →  1\nadd([4, 2])\nadd([4, 0])\ncount([2, 2])  →  2\ncount([0, 0])  →  0\ncount([1, 1])  →  0",
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "input": "DetectSquares()\nadd([5, 5])\nadd([5, 5])\ncount([5, 5])  →  0",
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Store how often each point was added: `points[x][y]` = count. Looking up whether a point exists is then O(1).",
  "An axis-aligned square through the query point `(x, y)` has another corner **straight above or below it**, at `(x, y2)`. That fixes the side length `|y2 − y|`.",
  "The remaining two corners must then be at `(x ± side, y)` and `(x ± side, y2)`. If they exist, the number of squares is the **product of the three corner counts** (duplicates give separate squares)."
 ],
 "code": "from collections import defaultdict\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass DetectSquares:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.points = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(int))\n\n    def add(self, point: List[int]) -> None:\n        x, y = point\n        self.points[x][y] += 1\n\n    def count(self, point: List[int]) -> int:\n        x, y = point\n        count = 0\n\n        for y2 in self.points[x]:\n            if y2 != y:\n                side_length = abs(y2 - y)\n\n                for x2 in (x + side_length, x - side_length):\n                    if x2 in self.points and y in self.points[x2]:\n                        count += (\n                            self.points[x2][y]\n                            * self.points[x2][y2]\n                            * self.points[x][y2]\n                        )\n\n        return count",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(1) add, O(n) count",
  "timeWhy": "`count` looks at every stored point that shares the query's x-coordinate.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "One counter per distinct point."
 },
